  "query_preview": "..\n\nFog development is following a similar temporal cadence to yesterday \nas noted via Nighttime Microphysics RGB. The geographic footprint of \nthe fog plume, however, is taking on a more southerly drift given \nthe backed wind direction in comparison to yesterday while also \nshowing signs of mechanical turbulence. Nonetheless, likely to see \nanother localized area of dense fog for the Thumb areas and \nnortheast suburbs as winds go calmer toward daybreak. MOS guidance \nis also still on track for another round of fog Tuesday morning, and \nmore bullish than the past few days as remnant moisture from the \ndeformation axis wraps around the high pressure center. This \ncoincides with the high pressure center moving overhead which will \nminimize gradient flow for optimized radiative cooling/fog \npotential. Fog lifts and mixes out by mid-morning both today and \nTuesday, extending the stretch of dry and seasonable fall weather \nfor SE Michigan. Daytime highs both days expected to peak in the low-\nmid 70s under plenty of sunshine. \n\nA pattern shift begins Tuesday on the western flank of the high \nnoted by a wind shift to the southwest. This leads to modest thermal \nadvection that peaks Tuesday night, boosting h8 temperatures from 10 \nC to 15 C by Wednesday morning. The first round of mid-level height \nfalls approaches early Wednesday to bring thickening mid-level cloud \nto the region. By this point the east coast system will be dislodged \nand the low levels will be characterized by the western flank of \nbroad eastern CONUS high pressure. This will be quite effective in \nstalling low level moisture transport to our west through much of \nthe day Wednesday, keeping the majority of SE Michigan dry but \ncloudy with virga. The trend toward a drier forecast Wednesday also \nboosts the temperature forecast for Wednesday, with highs expected \nto climb into the mid-upper 70s. \n\nRain chances then increase Wednesday night and persist through at \nleast Thursday as northern stream troughing phases with the broad \ndeformation/trough axis over central CONUS. The low level height \nfield tightens quickly Wednesday night as the trough approaches, \nleading to strengthening LLJ winds to about 50 knots by Thursday \nmorning. On its own this causes breezy SW flow across SE Michigan \nThursday where gusts to 25 mph are possible. The enhanced SW flow \nwill also enhanced moisture transport into lower Michigan, which \nwill be engaged by the deformation and approaching frontal forcing \nto bring rain to the region Thursday. Forcing will be defined \nprimarily by deformation and WAA while lapse rates stay moist \nadiabatic, so expecting a light-moderate stratiform nature to \nThursday's rain chances. That said, the impressive moisture axis \n(PWAT nearly 2.0\") will take most of the day to sink south across \nLower Michigan which could grind out 0.50-0.75\" when all is said and \ndone. That said, preceding dry weather and long duration rainfall \nlimits flooding concerns with this rain. \n\nA meaningful cool down is then expected Friday into the weekend post-\nfront, with showers tapering off Friday. There is still some \nuncertainty as to how aggressively high pressure pushes in behind \nthe fropa, which keeps low rain chances going through Friday. \nTemperature prospects for the weekend carry higher confidence, with \nhighs only reaching the mid 60s and lows back into the 40s to start \nOctober.",
